Sure, here's a simple implementation of a Connect Four game on a 4x4 board in Rust. This code includes basic game logic and handling player turns. It doesn't include features like checking for a win or handling a full board, but it should give you a good starting point.

```rust
use std::io;

fn main() {
    let mut board = [[0; 4]; 4];
    let mut current_player = 1;

    loop {
        print_board(&board);
        make_move(&mut board, current_player);
        current_player = 3 - current_player; // Switch between 1 and 2
    }
}

fn print_board(board: &[[i32; 4]; 4]) {
    for row in board {
        for &cell in row {
            print!("{} ", cell);
        }
        println!();
    }
}

fn make_move(board: &mut [[i32; 4]; 4], player: i32) {
    println!("Player {}'s turn", player);
    let mut column = 0;
    loop {
        println!("Enter column (0-3): ");
        let mut input = String::new();
        io::stdin().read_line(&mut input).expect("Failed to read line");
        column = input.trim().parse().expect("Please type a number!");
        if column < 0 || column > 3 {
            println!("Column out of range!");
        } else if board[0][column] != 0 {
            println!("Column full!");
        } else {
            for row in (0..4).rev() {
                if board[row][column] == 0 {
                    board[row][column] = player;
                    break;
                }
            }
            break;
        }
    }
}
```

This code sets up a simple game loop where players take turns entering column numbers to place their markers. It checks for valid inputs and updates the board accordingly. You can expand upon this by adding win conditions and handling a full board.
